  "query_preview": "../Friday through Wednesday/...Upper level features \ncontinue to show a rather active pattern through the long range \nperiod. We start out with the formation of a broad upper trough \nstretching from the Russian Far East to NW Canada on Friday. \nThe trough is anchored by an upper low moving out of Russia late \nweek that eventually positions itself over SW mainland Alaska. \nThe broad trough stays in place into early next week, but several \nshort waves are evident rotating around the southern edge of it. \nThere are indications that the trough may break down by mid next \nweek, but there is not much agreement on that scenario yet.\n\nThe active weather pattern is still apparent at the surface with a\nseries of fronts expected to move through the region late week,\nthrough the weekend, and into early next week. First the low and\nfront that is approaching on Friday is still there, but details \nare still being worked out. The trend so far with it is still \nsetting Friday up to be wet (up to 1 to 3 inches of rain Fri \nmorning to Sat morning) and breezy (winds of 15 to 25 kt for the \nSE gulf and inside waters) regardless. \n\nThe weekend into early next week is looking interesting as well \nwith a strong broad area of low pressure moving into the gulf from\nthe SW and heading toward the northern gulf coast. In addition to\nthe stronger onshore SW flow it will bring, it its wake is a shot\nof cooler air that will be coming in from the west. Forecast \nconvective indices are already indicating that Sunday into Monday \nmay be rather convective/showery in nature as the cooler air moves\nin and the potential is there for some thunderstorms across a \nlarge area of the gulf and outer coast at that time. Retained the \narea of slight chance thunder that was put in yesterday, but also \nadded some areas of chance of thunderstorms along the higher \nterrain along the outer coast. This system is also looking rather \nwet and breezy with strong SW to W flow across the gulf into the \npanhandle and an organized front enhancing the rainfall Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning.\n\nDamp weather continues into the first half of next week with \nonshore flow continuing, but showers and W to SW winds will not be\nas strong. There are indications of more systems moving into the \ngulf next week, but there is no consensus on when or where yet.",
